---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
const href = (route: string) => `${base}${route.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
const workflow = 'https://maxwell3919.github.io/DFT-Research-Workflow/operations/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methods"
  description="A conceptual map from electronic-structure questions to calculated physical objects."
  current="methods"
>
  <h1>Methods</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A method specifies which physical problem is reduced, which approximation is added, and which object is calculated. It is not an accuracy rank or a software choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Ground-state density methods and approximations</h2>
  <p>
    Ground-state DFT changes the basic variable to the density; <a href={href('/theory/kohn-sham-density-functional-theory/')}><strong>Kohn–Sham theory</strong></a> seeks an auxiliary noninteracting representation for a ground-state density that admits a suitable pure-state or ensemble noninteracting representation. Practical calculations then approximate exchange and correlation. LDA, GGA, and meta-GGA use progressively richer semilocal ingredients; hybrids use nonlocal exchange in a generalized Kohn–Sham operator. DFT+U adds a declared localized-subspace interaction, while dispersion corrections or nonlocal van der Waals functionals address long-range correlation. Each changes a different part of the model, and none is uniformly superior for every observable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spin-polarized and noncollinear formulations enlarge the density variables; spin–orbit coupling adds relativistic spin–orbital physics. They produce candidate magnetic states, spin textures, or anisotropy energies, not automatic proof of the global magnetic state or finite-temperature order. See <a href={href('/theory/exchange-correlation-functionals-and-approximations/')}>exchange–correlation approximations</a> and <a href={href('/theory/relativistic-electronic-structure-spin-and-magnetism/')}>spin and relativity</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Representation and core treatment</h2>
  <p>
    Plane waves and real-space grids offer systematic finite representations; localized orbitals trade that uniformity for compact, chemically adapted functions. All-electron methods treat core and valence explicitly. Pseudopotentials replace core states with an effective operator; PAW transforms smooth auxiliary orbitals and reconstructs all-electron-like quantities, usually within a frozen-core dataset. These choices control basis error, boundary conditions, transferability, accessible observables, and cost; they do not change the underlying approximation in the same way as choosing a functional. See <a href={href('/theory/discretization-and-basis-representations/')}>discretization</a>, <a href={href('/theory/plane-wave-and-real-space-methods/')}>plane-wave and real-space methods</a>, <a href={href('/theory/localized-orbital-methods/')}>localized orbitals</a>, and <a href={href('/theory/pseudopotentials-paw-and-core-valence-treatments/')}>core treatment</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Structures, forces, and paths</h2>
  <p>
    Energy derivatives give forces and stress. Geometry optimization searches for a stationary structure under declared constraints; stress is conjugate to strain, and its linear derivative defines an elastic tensor under a stated clamped-ion or relaxed-ion convention. A stationary point, positive local curvature, or elastic stability condition does not establish the global or finite-temperature stable phase. The nudged elastic band method instead optimizes a proposed path between specified endpoints and yields a barrier for that path, not a rate or proof that no lower path exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Vibrations, response, and finite temperature</h2>
  <p>
    Finite differences obtain derivatives from separately perturbed calculations; density-functional perturbation theory solves the corresponding linear-response problem. Atomic displacements give force constants and phonons; electric, strain, or other perturbations give their corresponding response tensors and coupling matrix elements. These results require a valid reference state, perturbation sector, harmonic or linear regime, and Brillouin-zone coverage. Ab initio molecular dynamics instead samples nuclear trajectories on an electronic energy surface; its output supports ensemble claims only after equilibration, correlation, finite-size, and sampling limits are addressed. See <a href={href('/theory/linear-response-and-excited-states/')}>linear response</a> and <a href={href('/theory/statistical-mechanics/')}>statistical mechanics</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Localized interpolation and topology</h2>
  <p>
    Wannier methods construct a localized representation when the selected subspace permits it, enabling dense interpolation, low-energy Hamiltonians, polarization, Berry quantities, and topology analysis. Topology or a required symmetry can obstruct a globally smooth, exponentially localized representation. The calculated object depends on the selected subspace, gauge, windows, and interpolation fidelity. Localization or band inversion alone is not a topological invariant; the protecting symmetry, gap, filling, and invariant must be stated. See <a href={href('/theory/berry-phases-and-electronic-topology/')}>Berry phases and topology</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Excitations and spectra</h2>
  <p>
    GW approximates the electronic self-energy to obtain charged addition and removal energies. The Bethe–Salpeter equation adds an electron–hole kernel to describe neutral excitations and optical spectra; time-dependent DFT obtains density response with an approximate time-dependent kernel. These methods calculate different objects: a quasiparticle gap, a neutral excitation, and a measured optical onset coincide only under additional conditions involving momentum, selection rules, matrix elements, and the experimental probe. See <a href={href('/theory/many-body-perturbation-theory-and-quasiparticles/')}>many-body perturbation theory</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Coupling and transport</h2>
  <p>
    Electron–phonon methods combine electronic states, phonons, and coupling matrix elements to estimate linewidths, relaxation, mass renormalization, or conventional superconducting quantities. Semiclassical Boltzmann methods map band velocities and a declared scattering model to transport coefficients. Both are composite calculations: dense interpolation and numerical convergence are necessary, but their physical assumptions and the target observable require separate validation.
  </p>

  <h2>Execution boundary</h2>
  <p>
    Atlas explains why a method is chosen, what it calculates, and where its interpretation fails. DFT Research Workflow covers <a href={`${workflow}choose-dft-method-and-computational-setup/`}>setup choice</a>, <a href={`${workflow}test-numerical-convergence/`}>observable convergence</a>, <a href={`${workflow}calculate-reference-ground-state/`}>reference states</a>, target calculations, and <a href={`${workflow}validate-results-and-scientific-conclusions/`}>supported conclusions</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Implementations and tools</h2>
  <p>
    After the method and calculated object are clear, use the <a href={href('/computational-tools/')}>Computational Tools map</a> to identify codes, data resources, post-processing tools, and workflow systems that implement part of that method stack. Tool availability is not a method-selection or validation criterion.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
